## Root cause

`(*responseWriter).WriteHeader` in /gin/response_writer.go warned about overriding an already-written status but then overrode it anyway:

```go
func (w *responseWriter) WriteHeader(code int) {
	if code > 0 && w.status != code {
		if w.Written() {
			debugPrint("[WARNING] Headers were already written. Wanted to override status code %d with %d", w.status, code)
		}
		w.status = code   // <-- still mutates w.status after the header was sent
	}
}
```

Once `Written()` is true the real status code has already been handed to `http.ResponseWriter` (via `WriteHeaderNow`, `Write`, `WriteString` or `Flush`), so the debug message is self-contradictory: the code must *not* be overridden. Because `w.status` was still mutated, `Status()` (and gin's logger / `serveError` logic, which read `Status()`) reported a status code that was never sent to the client — exactly the "second WriteHeader call overwriting the status code / Status() returning the wrong value after the handler wrote a status" defect. The missing early `return` is the whole bug; `WriteHeaderNow` idempotency, `Write`→`WriteHeaderNow`, `Size`/`Written` bookkeeping are all correct.

## Fix (only /gin/response_writer.go, +1 line)

```diff
@@ -61,6 +61,7 @@ func (w *responseWriter) WriteHeader(code int) {
 	if code > 0 && w.status != code {
 		if w.Written() {
 			debugPrint("[WARNING] Headers were already written. Wanted to override status code %d with %d", w.status, code)
+			return
 		}
 		w.status = code
 	}
```

## Verification

Throwaway test `/gin/zz_tmp_rw_check_test.go` (created, run, deleted) exercised: fresh writer state; `WriteHeader(201)` before any write (status remembered, recorder untouched); `WriteHeaderNow` flush + idempotency; `WriteHeader(500)` after the header was written; `Write`/`WriteString` without `WriteHeader` (implicit 200, Size counting); `WriteHeader` after an implicit write.

